# See device.te for why the PCI subtree is labelled rather than granted as
# plain sysfs. The path is the same on QEMU and on a real PC -- PCI segment 0
# is where both put the GPU.
genfscon sysfs /devices/pci0000:00 u:object_r:sysfs_pci:s0

# ACPI battery and AC adapter, for the health HAL.
#
# The kernel finds both -- "ACPI: battery: Slot [BAT0] (battery present)" and
# "ACPI: AC: AC Adapter [AC] (on-line)" -- but Android reported no battery at
# all ("healthd: No battery devices found", "battery none chg="), because
# healthd could not read them:
#     avc: denied { read } name="type" dev="sysfs" ino=15014
#          scontext=u:r:hal_health_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# Nothing needs to be *granted*: system/sepolicy/private/hal_health.te already
# carries r_dir_file(hal_health_server, sysfs_batteryinfo) and its comment says
# outright that "Vendors should mark sysfs_batteryinfo on all files read by
# health HAL service." This is that marking, and it is all that was missing.
#
# It has to be the /devices path rather than /sys/class/power_supply, because
# the entries there are symlinks and SELinux checks the label of the real
# inode. Enumerating the class directory itself is already allowed by
# "allow hal_health_server sysfs:dir r_dir_perms" in the same file.
#
# Labelling only the power_supply subdirectories, not the ACPI tree above them:
# a genfscon prefix relabels everything beneath it, and pointing it at
# /devices/LNXSYSTM would take the whole ACPI device tree away from the plain
# sysfs type that other domains expect.
#
# PNP0C0A is the ACPI battery device ID and ACPI0003 the AC adapter; slot 01 is
# listed for machines with a second battery bay. Paths that do not exist on a
# given machine are simply unused, so listing the variants costs nothing.
genfscon sysfs /devices/LNXSYSTM:00/LNXSYBUS:00/PNP0C0A:00/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/LNXSYSTM:00/LNXSYBUS:00/PNP0C0A:01/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/LNXSYSTM:00/LNXSYBUS:00/ACPI0003:00/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/LNXSYSTM:00/LNXSYBUS:00/ACPI0003:01/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0

# The AC adapter is NOT under LNXSYBUS with the battery -- it is on the platform
# bus. The diagnostic added to pc_kmsg_file.sh reported both real paths:
#     BAT0 : Battery : /sys/devices/LNXSYSTM:00/LNXSYBUS:00/PNP0C0A:00/power_supply/BAT0
#     AC   :         : /sys/devices/platform/ACPI0003:00/power_supply/AC
# The battery guess above was right and the AC guess was wrong, and the split
# shows in healthd: the battery came back ("battery l=98 v=13089", where it had
# been "battery none"), but the charger did not -- chg= stayed empty and the
# status stuck at DISCHARGING even on mains. Note AC's type field printed empty
# above while BAT0's printed "Battery"; that empty field IS the denial.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/ACPI0003:00/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/ACPI0003:01/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0

# The panel backlight, so display brightness can be changed.
#
# /sys/class/backlight/intel_backlight is a symlink into the DRM connector's
# directory, and SELinux resolves it to the real path, which sits inside the
# subtree labelled sysfs_pci for the GPU above. drm_hwcomposer could not
# traverse it:
#     avc: denied { search } name="pci0000:00" dev="sysfs"
#          scontext=u:r:hal_graphics_composer_default:s0
#          tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs_pci:s0 tclass=dir
#     E drmhwc: Could not read power from
#               /sys/class/backlight/intel_backlight/bl_power
#
# In the HWC3 model the composer owns display brightness -- drm_hwcomposer
# implements ExecuteSetDisplayBrightness and carries a sysfs backlight
# controller that reads bl_power, max_brightness, brightness and scale, and
# writes bl_power and brightness. So this is not the missing ILights HAL that
# LightsService complains about; that governs notification LEDs and the like.
#
# sysfs_leds is AOSP's own type for exactly this (public/file.te). AOSP ships
# no genfscon for it because the path is per-board; this is that entry.
# genfscon resolves by longest prefix, so this wins over the /devices/pci0000:00
# rule above without disturbing it.
genfscon sysfs /devices/pci0000:00/0000:00:02.0/drm/card0/card0-eDP-1/intel_backlight u:object_r:sysfs_leds:s0

# The GPU itself, so app processes can enumerate DRM devices.
#
# libdrm walks /sys/dev/char/226:128 -> device -> device/drm to build the list
# EGL then picks from, and every GL client does that in-process. Under the
# sysfs_pci label above, an untrusted_app cannot read it, so the list comes back
# empty and Mesa gives up before it ever opens the render node:
#     I MESA    : Using fallback gralloc implementation
#     W EGL-MAIN: Failed to open any DRM device
#     W EGL-MAIN: egl: failed to create dri2 screen
#     F HWUI    : Failed to create context, error = EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED
# which aborts every app that draws.
#
# The fix is not to widen sysfs_pci -- five neverallows refuse that, as
# private/pc_graphics.te records. sysfs_gpu is AOSP's own type for exactly this
# subtree, and its policy already grants the read to every GPU client:
#     private/gpu_sphal_domain.te:  r_dir_file(gpu_sphal_domain, sysfs_gpu)
# so labelling the device correctly grants apps what they need without a single
# new allow rule for them.
#
# Longest prefix still wins, so the intel_backlight rule below is unaffected and
# the rest of /devices/pci0000:00 stays sysfs_pci.
genfscon sysfs /devices/pci0000:00/0000:00:02.0 u:object_r:sysfs_gpu:s0

# ...and the path libdrm reaches it through. Labelling the GPU alone was not
# enough: xf86drm.c only ever looks at
#
#     /sys/dev/char/226:128
#     /sys/dev/char/226:128/device
#     /sys/dev/char/226:128/device/drm
#
# and /sys/dev/char is plain sysfs, which appdomain may not read. So the GPU
# node came back sysfs_gpu while the route to it did not, enumeration still
# returned nothing, and apps kept aborting on EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED.
#
# Narrower than it looks: what lives here is one symlink per char device named
# <major>:<minor>. Each symlink's target keeps its own label, so an app can see
# that 226:128 exists and follow it to the GPU -- already sysfs_gpu -- and gets
# nowhere on anything else. /class/drm has the same shape.
genfscon sysfs /dev/char u:object_r:sysfs_gpu:s0
genfscon sysfs /class/drm u:object_r:sysfs_gpu:s0
